.TH BIGINTADDUINT_32 3 "2026-09-25" "BigNums 3" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
bigIntAddUInt_32, bigIntMulUInt_32, bigIntDivUInt32, bigIntModUInt32 \- perform scalar integer arithmetic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <bignums.h>

int bigIntAddUInt_32(BigInt *a, uint32_t b);
int bigIntMulUInt_32(BigInt *a, uint32_t b);
int64_t bigIntDivUInt32(BigInt *a, uint32_t divisor);
int64_t bigIntModUInt32(const BigInt *a, uint32_t divisor);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Addition and multiplication grow storage as needed, reserving before mutation.
The BigInt operand is signed; the uint32_t scalar remains nonnegative.
Addition can cross zero. Nonzero scalar multiplication preserves the input sign.
Multiplication by zero produces canonical zero and retains capacity.
Division truncates toward zero and returns a remainder with the dividend's sign.
For positive divisor d, a = q*d + r and |r| < d. For example, -7 divided by 3
produces quotient -2 and remainder -1.
Modulo returns a remainder without modifying the input.
A zero divisor leaves the input unchanged.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Addition and multiplication return 0, -1 for allocation failure, or INT_MAX
for an unrepresentable size. Errors leave the value unchanged.
Division and modulo return int64_t remainders. A zero divisor returns INT64_MIN,
which is outside the valid remainder range, without changing the input.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
